   "output": "You are comparing two drafts written from the same brief. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; judge only the text below.\n\nBrief: Write the docs-site page for the `term-weave` skill with the sections What it does, When to use it, How it works, and What you get.\n\n<<<DRAFT A\n# term-weave\n\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a supplied folder. Every drafted definition links to the passage that supports it.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun it manually with:\n\n```text\n/term-weave <source-folder>\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types. If the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\nIt does not invent definitions. Terms without a supporting explanation go under **Unresolved terms**. When sources give conflicting definitions, it preserves both in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\n\n## What you get\n\nA `GLOSSARY.md` file in the working directory, with glossary entries sorted alphabetically and **Unresolved terms** after the defined entries. The source documents remain unchanged.\nDRAFT A>>>\n\n<<<DRAFT B\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` is a skill that drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a folder you supply.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun it manually with `/term-weave <source-folder>`.\n\nIllustrative example:\n\n```text\n/term-weave ./project-docs\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types. If the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\nEvery drafted definition links to the passage that supports it. The skill does not invent definitions. Terms without a supporting explanation appear under **Unresolved terms**.\n\nIf sources give conflicting definitions, it preserves both in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe skill writes `GLOSSARY.md` in your working directory. It sorts defined entries alphabetically and places **Unresolved terms** after them. It never changes the source documents.\nDRAFT B>>>\n\nAnswer each question with \"A\", \"B\", or \"SAME\":\n- \"reader\": Could a first-time reader do what they came to do?\n- \"careful\": Does it read like a careful person wrote it?\n- \"overall\": Which draft is better overall?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"reader\": \"A\", \"careful\": \"A\", \"overall\": \"A\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}",
